{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff -- gen.py && cat ANSWER.md'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/gen.py b/gen.py\nindex be0b3bc..4aeba5a 100755\n--- a/gen.py\n+++ b/gen.py\n@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@ import sys\n def hr_target(step):\n     \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n     lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n-    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n+    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n \n \n def build_step(step):\n# HR range fix\n\nChanged `gen.py` to encode each planned HR corridor as `hr: {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": low, \"end\": high}`. The previous payload used `value` for the lower bound; in Intervals.icu's workout format, `value` denotes a single target, while `start` and `end` denote a range. That explains why the accepted event did not preserve the planned corridor on the watch.\n\nThe plan still specifies percentages of LTHR, so Intervals can resolve them using the athlete's threshold. I did not push a new event, so the watch display has not been verified. The existing event and readback remain a record of the old payload.\n\nSchema reference: [Intervals.icu workout builder example showing an `%lthr` HR range with `start` and `end`](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163/1123).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
